 accounts and net expenditures of wholly owned Government corporations, etc., except as indicated in footnote 6. Expenditure classifications in these tables are based on the Daily Treasury Statement, and therefore differ somewhat from those in the 1949 Budget.\\n8/ Figures for fiscal year 1948 include $3,000 million transfer to Foreign Economic Cooperation Trust Fund; figures for fiscal year 1949 exclude expenditures from this Fund.\\n9/ Includes public works undertaken by the Votorens Administration.\\n10/ Established in accordance with the Economic Cooperation Act of 1948. Negative figures indicate receipts.\\n11/ See footnote 12.\\n12/ Department of the Army expenditures include expenditures on behalf of the Department of the Air Force which are made out of appropriations to the Department of the Army, but exclude expenditures for the following: payments under the Armed Forces Leave Act, UNRRA, and surplus property disposal, shown elsewhere in Table 3; international finance and aid, shown in Table 4; river and harbor work and flood control, included under \\\"Public works\\\" in Table 5; and Panama Canal. War expenditures of the Panama Canal, all prior to July 1, 1947, are included under \\\"Other\\\" in Table 3; other expenditures of the Panama Canal are included under \\\"Miscellaneous\\\" in Table 5.\\n13/ Department of the Navy expenditures except those for payments under the Armed Forces Leave Act, UNRRA, and surplus property disposal, shown elsewhere in Table 3, and those for international finance and aid, shown in Table 4.\\n14/ Exclude expenditures for UNRRA and surplus property disposal shown elsewhere in Table 3.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"footnote\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [805, 797, 1493, 1706], \"page_id\": 16}], \"content\": \"15/ Beginning March 1948, includes reimbursement for expenditures by the Departments of the Army and the Navy for care and handling of surplus property overseas.\\n16/ National defense and related activities of F. F. C. and its affiliates. After July 1, 1947, expenditures for these purposes were not segregated from the other expenditures of the Corporation and its affiliates, which are included under \\\"Miscellaneous\\\" expenditures in Table 5.\\n17/ For content before July 1948 see \\\"Treasury Bulletin\\\" for February 1948, page 7, and September 1948, page 4. Beginning July 1948 consists of expenditures of the Selective Service System and the Office of the Secretary of Defense.\\n18/ Excludes expenditures under Economic Cooperation Administration, shown elsewhere in Table 4.\\n19/ Figures for fiscal year 1943 include $3,000 million transfer to Foreign Economic Cooperation Trust Fund; figures for fiscal year 1949 exclude expenditures from this Fund. Figures exclude expenditures for international finance and aid shown elsewhere in Table 4.\\n20/ Consists of expenditures of International Children's Emergency Fund, and loan for construction and furnishing of United Nations headquarters.\\n21/ Department of Agriculture expenditures except those for UNRRA, surplus property disposal, other national defense and related activities prior to July 1947; international finance and aid; and forest roads and trails, included under \\\"Public works\\\" in Table 5.\\n22/ For more detail of these expenditures, see Tables 7, 5, and 9. Tax refunds shown there are excluded from expenditures in Table 5 and included in Table 1 under \\\"Refunds of taxes and duties\\\". Expenditures for the U. S. Employment Service during the fiscal years 1943 through 1946 are likewise excluded from Table 5 and included under \\\"Other\\\" in Table 3 as national defense expenditures.\\n23/ Federal Works Agency expenditures except those for surplus property disposal, other national defense and related activities prior to July 1947, and assistance to Greece and Turkey. Includes also river and harbor work and flood control, forest roads and trails, Bureau of Reclamation, and Tennessee Valley Authority.\