ulletin\\\" for March 1944, page 58; for the composition of the high-grade corporate bond average, see the \\\"Treasury Bulletin\\\" for July 1947, page 59, and January 1945, page 56. Treasury bond yields are computed on the basis of the mean of closing bid and ask quotations in the over-the-counter market, beginning September 2, 1941. Prior to that time, yields were computed on the basis of closing prices on the New York Stock Exchange, except that on days when an issue did not sell the yield was computed on the mean of closing bid and ask quotations on the Exchange. Corporate bond yields are computed on the basis of closing prices on the New York Stock\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 14, \"type\": \"footnote\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [799, 1614, 1403, 1850], \"page_id\": 50}], \"content\": \"1/ Exchange and the New York Curb Exchange; on days when an issue does not sell, the price of the last preceding sale is used. For definition of taxable and partially tax-exempt bonds, see footnotes to the \\\"Treasury Survey of Ownership\\\" in this issue of the \\\"Treasury Bulletin\\\". The partially tax-exempt series was discontinued on December 15, 1945, because there were no longer any bonds in this category with fifteen or more years to maturity or first call.\\n2/ Average revised beginning November 1, 1944. Old average for November 1944 was 2.564.\\n3/ Average revised beginning April 1, 1947. Old average for April 1947 was 2.478.\\n4/ Market closed.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 15, \"type\": \"footnote\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [62, 20, 240, 59], \"page_id\": 51}], \"content\": \"January 1949\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [86, 234, 141, 1670], \"page_id\": 51}], \"content\": \"AVERAGE YIELDS OF LONG-TERM TREASURY AND CORPORATE BONDS\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [139, 834, 177, 1068], \"page_id\": 51}], \"content\": \"Monthly Series\", \"desc